+# Daily Grind performance monitoring
+
+The homepage loads `performance-monitor.js` as a deferred module, without changing
+link behavior or the page's appearance. It observes native
+`interaction-contentful-paint` entries for navigation links, Order Now, and each
+Seasonal Favorites card (including its View Details link). Stable
+`data-performance-id` attributes identify these components.
+
+Each interaction report includes the component, interaction ID, and `duration` in
+milliseconds from input start to presentation of newly painted content. Larger
+contentful updates can generate further reports for the same interaction. These
+are contentful paint candidates, not a claim that all async work has completed.
+Event Timing IDs associate inputs with paints; captured trusted pointer/keyboard
+timestamps cover fast inputs below Event Timing's reporting threshold.
+
+For client-side view navigations, the monitor observes `soft-navigation` entries,
+seeds LCP with `getLargestInteractionContentfulPaint()`, and updates it only for
+paints with the same interaction ID. Navigation reports contain the destination,
+FCP and LCP durations, and content size. They finalize on the next soft navigation,
+when the document becomes hidden, or on `pagehide`; pending records are drained.
+
+## Reading reports
+
+`window.dailyGrindPerformance.supported` exposes browser capabilities and
+`window.dailyGrindPerformance.reports` retains the latest 200 reports locally.
+An analytics integration can subscribe to new reports:
+
+```js
+window.addEventListener('daily-grind:performance', ({ detail }) => {
+  // Forward `detail` to your existing analytics collector here.
+});
+```
+
+No analytics endpoint is configured and no reports are sent over the network.
+Both paint APIs are feature-detected independently of Event Timing support;
+unsupported browsers skip those observers without affecting the page. No
+approximate frame or URL-change timings are presented as contentful paints.
+
+The existing links perform full document navigations, and this repository has no
+client-side router or destination pages. A link that unloads the homepage cannot
+produce a destination paint in the homepage's timeline. Measuring that destination
+requires monitoring on the destination page. The soft-navigation observer will
+measure actual client-side view changes if a router is added; it does not intercept
+links or manufacture view changes.
+
+## Checks
+
+Run `node --test performance-monitor.test.cjs` for isolated observer and lifecycle
+tests. These use mocked performance entries and do not start a server or browser.
